  "title": "Indicative verb tenses",
  "lesson": {
    "kind": "review",
    "focusPoint": null,
    "points": [
      {
        "id": "p1",
        "heading": "Theme overview",
        "body": "Indicative presents facts as real. This week you mapped ten tenses: five simple forms and five compounds with auxiliary + participle.\n\nTogether they cover present, past, future, and hypothesis with or without perfect aspect. Knowing the list is step one; using it with judgment is step two."
      },
      {
        "id": "p2",
        "heading": "Point 1 overview",
        "body": "Five simple tenses: present, imperfect, preterite, future, conditional. They are immediate time anchors; imperfect gives background and preterite closes the action.\n\nWithout this base, compounds only add confusion. Review one full conjugation of a regular verb."
      },
      {
        "id": "p3",
        "heading": "Point 2 overview",
        "body": "Five compounds: present perfect, pluperfect, anterior preterite, future perfect, conditional perfect. All share a participle; the auxiliary marks temporal location.\n\nRemember: the participle does not change for person. You conjugate the auxiliary."
      },
      {
        "id": "p4",
        "heading": "Point 3 overview",
        "body": "Choice depends on temporal relation and aspect: finished at a point, still relevant, before another past, or hypothetical?\n\nAlways ask for the time anchor before you choose the form."
      },
      {
        "id": "p5",
        "heading": "Point 1 synthesis",
        "body": "Without clear simple tenses, compounds confuse. Master present / imperfect / preterite / future / conditional first; then add auxiliary + participle with the same time-anchor logic.\n\nReturn to the five sentences from the simple-tense deepen day and check they are still correct."
      }
    ],
    "summary": ""
  },
